title: First come, first served
mode: vhdl
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
---- intro
`when ... else` can chain: the first condition that holds wins.

```
y <= a when s1 = '1' else
     b when s0 = '1' else
     c;
```

"`y` is `a` if `s1` is on; otherwise `b` if `s0` is on; otherwise `c`." This is a **priority** chooser: `s1` beats `s0`, even when both are on.
---- task
Make `y` be `a` while `s1` is 1; else `b` while `s0` is 1; else `c`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity priority is
  port(
    a  : in  std_logic;
    b  : in  std_logic;
    c  : in  std_logic;
    s1 : in  std_logic;
    s0 : in  std_logic;
    y  : out std_logic
  );
end priority;

architecture rtl of priority is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity priority is
  port(
    a  : in  std_logic;
    b  : in  std_logic;
    c  : in  std_logic;
    s1 : in  std_logic;
    s0 : in  std_logic;
    y  : out std_logic
  );
end priority;

architecture rtl of priority is
begin
    y <= a when s1 = '1' else
         b when s0 = '1' else
         c;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two `when`s and two `else`s, with `c` at the very end.
---- hint2
`y <= a when s1 = '1' else b when s0 = '1' else c;`
---- solution
```
y <= a when s1 = '1' else
     b when s0 = '1' else
     c;
```
Turn on both `s1` and `s0`: `y` follows `a`, because `s1` is checked first.
---- done
Order matters in a chain: swap the first two lines and `s0` wins instead. Interrupts in a processor are handled by priority like this.
